WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.008
The next morning Mary woke up when a young housemaid came into her room.

2
00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:14.775
While the young maid cleaned up the cinders in the fireplace, Mary lay in bed and looked around the gloomy room.

3
00:00:14.776 --> 00:00:20.759
The walls were covered with strange tapestries embroidered with forest scenes.

4
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.276
Through a window she could see a great stretch of land.

5
00:00:24.277 --> 00:00:28.200
It looked like an endless, dull purple sea.

6
00:00:28.201 --> 00:00:32.948
"What is that?" Mary pointed out the window.

7
00:00:32.949 --> 00:00:37.685
"That's the moor," said the housemaid with a grin. "Do you like it?"

8
00:00:37.686 --> 00:00:40.952
"No. I hate it."

9
00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:43.964
"That's because you're not used to it.

10
00:00:43.965 --> 00:00:48.589
You think it's too big and bare now. But you will like it."

11
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.760
"Do you like it?"

12
00:00:50.761 --> 00:00:53.871
"I do," said the housemaid.

13
00:00:53.872 --> 00:00:58.908
"In the spring it's covered with growing things that smell sweet.

14
00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:03.277
There's lots of fresh air, and the sky looks so high.

15
00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:07.239
I wouldn't live away from the moor for anything."

16
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.188
As the maid lit the fire, Mary listened to her with a puzzled expression.

17
00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:15.277
Servants in India were not like this.

18
00:01:15.278 --> 00:01:20.906
Her nanny and the others did not talk to their masters in such a friendly way.

19
00:01:20.907 --> 00:01:26.158
"What's your name?" Mary asked. "Are you going to be my servant?"

20
00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:31.494
"I'm Martha. I'll do the housemaid's work up here and wait on you a bit.

21
00:01:31.495 --> 00:01:34.366
But you won't need much waiting on."

22
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:38.207
"Who is going to dress me?" demanded Mary.

23
00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:43.111
Martha looked at Mary in amazement. "Can't you dress yourself?"

24
00:01:43.112 --> 00:01:46.979
"No. My nanny dressed me, of course."

25
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.434
"Well," said Martha bluntly, "it's time you learned."

26
00:01:52.435 --> 00:01:55.737
Martha helped Mary get dressed that morning.

27
00:01:55.738 --> 00:02:00.603
Then they went into the next room, where she set out Mary's breakfast on a table.

28
00:02:00.604 --> 00:02:03.841
"I don't want it," said Mary.

29
00:02:03.842 --> 00:02:08.287
"You don't want your porridge!" Martha couldn't believe this.

30
00:02:08.288 --> 00:02:12.896
"You don't know how good it is. Put a bit of sugar on it."

31
00:02:12.897 --> 00:02:15.270
But Mary refused.

32
00:02:15.271 --> 00:02:23.127
"If my brothers and sisters were here," said Martha, "they'd clean this table bare in five minutes."

33
00:02:23.128 --> 00:02:24.675
"Why?"

34
00:02:24.676 --> 00:02:29.888
"Because they never have enough to eat. I am one of 12 children.

35
00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:33.786
My father gets only a small wage each week.

36
00:02:33.787 --> 00:02:40.349
The children play on the moor all day, and my mother says the air of the moor fattens them up.

37
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:45.129
She thinks they must eat the grass the same as the wild ponies do.

38
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:51.056
My brother Dickon is 12 years old, and he has a young pony he calls his own."

39
00:02:51.057 --> 00:02:54.485
"Where did he get the pony?" asked Mary.

40
00:02:54.486 --> 00:02:59.091
She was amazed at how this servant talked on and on.

41
00:02:59.092 --> 00:03:02.414
"Dickon found it on the moor with its mother.

42
00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:09.027
He gave it bread and new grass, so now it follows him around and lets him sit on its back.

43
00:03:09.028 --> 00:03:12.666
Dickon's a kind lad, and animals like him."

44
00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:17.563
Mary had never had a pet of her own, but she'd always wanted one.

45
00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:23.093
She began to be a little interested in Dickon. This was something new.

46
00:03:23.094 --> 00:03:27.646
The only person she'd ever been interested in before was herself.

47
00:03:27.647 --> 00:03:31.435
She drank some tea and ate some toast with marmalade.

48
00:03:31.436 --> 00:03:36.030
"You wrap up warmly and run out to play now," said Martha.

49
00:03:36.031 --> 00:03:40.437
"It'll do you good and give you an appetite."

50
00:03:40.438 --> 00:03:43.552
Mary went to the window and looked out.

51
00:03:43.553 --> 00:03:49.657
She saw gardens and paths and big trees, but everything looked dull and wintry.

52
00:03:49.658 --> 00:03:52.889
"Why should I go out on a day like this?"

53
00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:58.924
"Well, if you don't go out, you'll have to stay in, and what is there to do here?"

54
00:03:58.925 --> 00:04:04.735
Mary looked around. There was nothing to do. "But who will go out with me?"

55
00:04:04.736 --> 00:04:07.838
"You'll have to learn to play by yourself.

56
00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:13.066
Our Dickon goes off on the moor and plays by himself for hours.

57
00:04:13.067 --> 00:04:16.237
That's how he made friends with the pony.

58
00:04:16.238 --> 00:04:22.866
He has sheep on the moor that know him and birds that come and eat from his hand."

59
00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:28.986
It was this mention of Dickon that made Mary decide to go out, though she was not aware of it.

60
00:04:28.987 --> 00:04:31.768
Perhaps she would see some birds.

61
00:04:31.769 --> 00:04:37.434
They would be different from those in India, and it might amuse her to look at them.

62
00:04:37.435 --> 00:04:40.761
Martha found Mary's coat and hat for her.

63
00:04:40.762 --> 00:04:44.800
Then Martha showed Mary the way downstairs and outside.

64
00:04:44.801 --> 00:04:52.385
"If you go around that way, you'll come to the gardens," she said, pointing to a gate in a wall of shrubbery.

65
00:04:52.386 --> 00:04:58.743
"One of the gardens is locked up. No one has been in it for ten years."

66
00:04:58.744 --> 00:05:00.600
"Why?"

67
00:05:00.601 --> 00:05:06.395
"Mr. Craven shut it when his wife died suddenly. It was her garden.

68
00:05:06.396 --> 00:05:12.092
He locked the door and then buried the key so no one could go inside.

69
00:05:12.093 --> 00:05:15.977
There's Mrs. Medlock's bell ringing. I must run."

70
00:05:15.978 --> 00:05:27.846
And with that Martha was off, leaving Mary to wander outside by herself.

